---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SeaBreak from '../../components/SeaBreak.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CTABand from '../../components/CTABand.astro';
import { celestial, ocean } from '../../config/images';
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Explore Astrology', href: '/explore/' },
  { label: 'Eclipses', href: '/explore/eclipses/' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Eclipses in Astrology, what they mark and how often"
  description="Solar and lunar eclipses occur near the lunar nodes in repeating seasons. Learn the astronomy, the astrological symbolism, and why an eclipse is context rather than a verdict."
  path="/explore/eclipses/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />
  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Explore Astrology</p>
        <h1>Eclipses</h1>
        <p class="lede">There is something extraordinary about watching the ordinary geometry of Sun, Moon, and Earth briefly line up. Astrology adds interpretation to that cycle, but the first thing worth doing is simply understanding what is happening.</p>
      </div>
      <img class="hero__img" src={ocean.lightBreakingStorm.src} srcset={ocean.lightBreakingStorm.srcset} sizes="100vw" alt={ocean.lightBreakingStorm.alt} width={ocean.lightBreakingStorm.width} height={ocean.lightBreakingStorm.height} fetchpriority="high" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What makes a moon an eclipse</h2>
      <p>
        A <a href="/explore/glossary/#new-moon">new moon</a> happens every month when the Moon
        passes between Earth and Sun. Most months the alignment is not exact because the Moon's
        orbit is tilted about five degrees from the plane of Earth's orbit. When a new or full moon
        occurs close enough to one of the <a href="/explore/lunar-nodes/">lunar nodes</a>, the
        geometry lines up for an eclipse.
      </p>
      <p>
        That requirement is why eclipses cluster into seasons rather than appearing randomly. The
        cycle is regular enough to predict and dramatic enough to have attracted human attention for
        a very long time.
      </p>

      <h2>The cycles worth knowing</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>About every six months:</strong> another eclipse season arrives.</li>
        <li><strong>About every 18–19 months:</strong> the lunar nodes change signs, shifting the zodiacal axis along which eclipses occur.</li>
        <li><strong>Over longer repeating cycles:</strong> eclipses return in related astronomical families, which is one reason astrologers sometimes compare a present eclipse with earlier periods.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SeaBreak image={celestial.distanceUnderEclipse} />

  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container prose flow">
      <h2>What astrologers look for</h2>
      <p>
        Astrologically, eclipses are often treated as intensified lunations and as markers of
        transition, culmination, emergence, or changing emphasis. A solar eclipse begins with a new
        moon; a lunar eclipse with a full moon. Those distinctions matter, but they are only the
        beginning of interpretation.
      </p>
      <p>
        What interests me more is where an eclipse lands in a particular chart, which natal points
        it contacts, how exact those contacts are, and what else is already happening in that life.
        The same eclipse can be central for one person and barely noticeable for another.
      </p>

      <h2>How I prefer to hold eclipse season</h2>
      <p>
        I do not think an eclipse needs fear around it. I also do not think every eclipse needs to
        be turned into a ritual or a prediction. It can be enough to notice what is becoming more
        visible, what is shifting, and which questions seem to have acquired more weight than they
        had a month ago.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sometimes the significance is obvious immediately. Sometimes it makes more sense in
        retrospect. And sometimes an eclipse is simply an eclipse in the sky. Astrology is more
        useful when we allow all three possibilities.
      </p>
      <p>
        See <a href="/current-sky/">the current eclipse dates</a>, or read about
        <a href="/explore/lunar-nodes/">the lunar nodes</a> that make them possible.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABand
    heading="Where does an eclipse meet your chart?"
    body="The date is collective. The house, aspects, and larger pattern are what make the question personal."
    buttonLabel="See the readings"
    buttonHref="/readings/"
  />
</BaseLayout>
